-- #1182, module 2 of 2. Declares interface `A2`, whose method is ALSO named `m`.
--
-- The entry imports only the INTERFACE NAME (`import a2.{A2}`), never the method name, so
-- `A2.m` is not bound to any spelling in the entry. It reaches the selector only through
-- the impl universe — which is exactly the point: `matchingEntries` keys candidates by
-- bare METHOD NAME, so this impl still lands in the candidate set for an occurrence that
-- names A1's method.
--
-- ⚠️ Importing `m` from BOTH modules instead is a different program and does NOT reproduce
-- this: it is rejected up front with `Ambiguous occurrence: 'm' is exported by both 'a1'
-- and 'a2'` (measured on this branch). The single-name import is what keeps the defect
-- reachable.
export interface A2 a where
  m : a -> Int
